<h2 id="snowball">
Snowball: the 256K release</h2>
<p>This comparison uses <a
href="https://huggingface.co/open-athena/Marin-Snowball-67B-A2B-v0.1.1/tree/59852d6f07261722bbec1edb6ab148317156b58e">Marin
Snowball 67B-A2B v0.1.1, revision 59852d6f07261722bbec1edb6ab148317156b58e</a>. Its model configuration
declares <strong>262,144 tokens</strong>; the live server registry confirms that same context length. Exact
tokenisation of all 88 full prompts ranges from {{ snowball_min_input }} to {{ snowball_max_input }} input
tokens. Every full prompt in the revised panel is admitted, and the response allowance is the full remaining
context after exact server tokenisation.</p>
<p>The October report used the older Step38 checkpoint served at 32,768 tokens, with a 40-question
context-fitting subset. Those historical scores describe that earlier model and serving setup. They are not a
context limit or result for v0.1.1. This report compares v0.1.1 across all 88 questions. It runs in BF16 on
two H100 GPUs per replica using expert parallelism, the supplied tokenizer, the grug reasoning parser and
Marin vLLM 0.0.0.dev20260924+marin.01911be34fac.cu132. The November 9 experimental plumbing checkpoint is not
used. Historical scores are not directly comparable to this revision because both prompts and model conditions
changed.</p>

{% if has_mochi %}
<h2 id="mochi">Mochi: Hero SFT step 400407</h2>
<p>Mochi uses <code>hero-sft-step400407</code> in BF16 with a 262,144-token context. All 88 unchanged
prompts fit, consuming 12,671–63,024 tokens and leaving 199,120–249,473 tokens for thinking and the answer.
Temperature is explicitly <strong>0.7</strong>, with thinking enabled, top-p 1.0 and top-k filtering disabled.
Seeds are derived from the frozen question IDs. These settings were chosen before collecting scores.</p>
<p>The OpenAI-compatible Marin endpoint receives <code>tools: null</code> and <code>tool_choice: none</code>.
The client never executes tools or calls the dashboard's web-search route. Raw responses retain the thinking
delimiters; the versioned adapter removes the complete reasoning block before applying the unchanged scorer.
An unfinished reasoning block cannot supply a final answer. Structured and textual tool-call events are audited.</p>
<p>The main collection uses up to eight concurrent requests; transport recovery uses up to four. An initial I01 stream
ended after 3,600.6 seconds without a finish event or token accounting, consistent with Iris's one-hour relay
timeout. Interrupted requests retain their partial streams and receive a separate retry with the same full
token budget. Completed answers are never rerun.</p>
<p>Live metadata confirms the model identifier, BF16 dtype, context limit and checkpoint path. The operator
reports Marin vLLM <code>0.0.0.dev20261113+marin.0ac9dcb157ee</code> on 16 GB200 GPUs with TP1/DP16
and expert parallelism. The tokenizer is reported as <code>marin-community/marin-tokenizer-tools</code> at
<code>3f6879acdaf3dae7874b2d48c7d11b9d8c6faf09</code>; exact prompt token IDs and counts are captured
from the live server template. The <a href="https://github.com/marin-community/marin/issues/11430">backend
investigation</a> documents load-dependent numerical differences, so fixed seeds do not establish bitwise replay.</p>
<p><a href="https://openathena.ai/pdbthink/reports/20261116-before-mochi/">Report before adding Mochi</a></p>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="audit">
Review of the recent main changes</h2>
<p>The November 9 correction makes interface questions explicitly protein-only, exposes nonstandard residue
identifier mappings, rejects clashes with unknown covalent topology, and corrects accepted contact sets and
displayed-coordinate gold in the historical mechanistic track. Its version bumps preserve the distinction from
earlier experiments.</p>
<p>All 88 published coordinate questions were reconstructed from their exact displayed coordinates and checked
with the current oracles. All remain admissible and all gold answers agree. All 88 system instructions and 43
user prompts change, so every model is evaluated afresh; historical responses are not relabelled. The old
data-loading path also silently substituted the current system instruction into historical datasets, and the
old migration helper could claim the latest prompt version after only editing a suffix. The update preserves
frozen system messages and rejects incomplete scientific-contract migrations, with regression tests.</p>

{% if comparison %}
<h2 id="budgets">
Output limits and earlier attempts</h2>

<p>The earlier GLM high-effort condition issued {{ comparison.requested_questions }} full requests: {{
comparison.uncapped_results }} uncapped results ({{ comparison.uncapped_correct }} correct), {{
comparison.provider_clamps }} responses clamped at 131,072 output tokens despite a 943,718-token request, and
{{ comparison.uncollected }} uncollected requests. That condition was stopped and preserved. The headline uses
a separate, uniform maximum-effort run across all 88 questions, following <a
href="https://openrouter.ai/z-ai/glm-5.3">GLM's documented default</a>. Earlier results, actual token counts
and hashes remain in the evidence; the provider clamp is not labelled as a native model limit.</p>

<p>The maximum-effort Parasail run also encountered the same 131,072-token provider clamp. A GMICloud probe
stopped at that same boundary with no final answer, despite reporting <code>stop</code>; it is also retained
as a collection failure. Changing reasoning effort or trying that route did not remove the cutoff. Recovery
uses a separate direct GLM FP8 endpoint with a documented 262,144-token total context, allocating its full
remaining context to each answer. When the direct endpoint went offline with one I01 response still
uncollected, that question moved to Fireworks through OpenRouter with maximum reasoning effort, temperature
zero and its full advertised 943,718-token output allowance in a 1,048,576-token context. The first Fireworks
stream ended without a finish event or token accounting; it remains a provider interruption. Two subsequent
attempts on the <code>fireworks/fast</code> priority route used the same budget. One failed with a provider
connection error; the other also ended without a finish event or token accounting. The I01 response remains
uncollected and contributes to GLM's displayed bounds, rather than being scored incorrect. Fireworks does not
expose an immutable weight revision or verified quantization. The evidence preserves every hosted attempt
and recovery condition; a provider change is not treated as proof of identical numerical inference.</p>

{% endif %}
<h3>Qwen: the native 256K condition</h3>
<p>All 88 Qwen questions use a 262,144-token context. One S05 response exhausted its full
206,951-token output allowance after a 55,193-token prompt and is recorded as a context-limit
truncation. The YaRN extension is outside this comparison's fixed 256K Qwen condition;
no larger-context answer replaces the native result.</p>

<h2 id="method">
Methods and provenance</h2>
<p>One initial attempt per question, with provider-failure and output-capacity recovery recorded separately. No prompt is shortened to fit
a model. Hosted endpoints use their verified maximum output allowance when it fits the exact prompt;
self-hosted models receive their full remaining context. Reasoning tokens count towards the allowance. Actual
input and output tokens, requested budgets, finish reasons and response hashes are retained in the evidence.
Endpoint output limits, remaining-context limits and collection failures are recorded separately; reaching a
lower provider clamp is not accepted as a native limit. Tools and the OpenRouter web plugin are explicitly
disabled, and raw responses are audited for tool events. No runner executes tool calls.</p>
<p>OpenRouter routes requests to inference providers such as Fireworks. The separate direct GLM
deployment is accessed without OpenRouter; it is not Z.ai's official API.</p>
<p>Sampling settings differ between models. Snowball uses temperature 0.6 and Qwen uses 1.0;
both use <code>top_p=0.95</code>, <code>top_k=20</code> and seed <code>20260929</code>.
GLM uses temperature 0.0 across its primary and recovery routes. Astra, Opus, Kimi and DeepSeek omit
temperature, top-p, top-k and seed, leaving sampling to their providers; their effective numeric defaults
were not verified. These are comparisons of the recorded configurations, not a temperature-matched study.</p>
<p>Qwen's sampling follows its pinned publisher recommendation for general thinking tasks. Snowball's 0.6
setting is an explicit run choice without a recommendation in its pinned release files; GLM's zero-temperature
condition is a greedy baseline. This report does not establish the optimal sampling settings for these models.</p>
<p>GLM 5.3 uses maximum reasoning effort: Parasail FP8 for the primary run, with GMICloud and the direct FP8
endpoint recorded as recovery routes, followed by Fireworks for the final uncollected question. An earlier
self-hosted batch queue admitted no metering requests;
the later direct recovery uses the documented default service tier. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 uses Fireworks;
the native route was unavailable under
the account’s data policy. Provider routing and reasoning settings remain part of each model condition. Equal
questions do not imply equal inference compute. Results are descriptive for this frozen panel; shared source
structures mean questions are not independent proteins. The predecessor’s questions and gold answers were
publicly released, so these scores describe a previously exposed panel. Fresh prompts, answers and raw
responses remain in the private run archive; the public report contains aggregates and hashes.</p>

